00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:15.990
do you know if the authors
tried to reinfect again

00:00:15.990 --> 00:00:18.290
after a longer timeframe,

00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:20.350
has there been any maybe

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.070
since this is a bioRxiv paper,

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:26.230
we often see some changes over some time.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.900
Do you know if maybe they
started with a shorter timeframe

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:31.110
and then it increased it

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.960
and then added some
addition to it later on?

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.140
Is there any information around that?

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.990
- Yes so I mean, as gene is noted,

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:42.590
often these papers on bioRxiv are modified

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:46.200
or changed or attitude before
they get finally published.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.840
I do know that this paper
was updated once already

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.780
and actually some more monkeys
were added to the study,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.420
so I would expect that this
lab is probably ongoing,

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:59.070
and is doing these sorts
of experiments now.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.116
But they haven't published that data yet.

00:01:01.116 --> 00:01:02.940
So we don't know the answer
to those questions yet.

00:01:04.423 --> 00:01:06.863
- Be really interesting
to see when they do.

00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:09.470
Okay, well first of all,

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.430
that was a really lovely way
of explaining immune memory.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:13.300
I really enjoyed that.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.510
Thank you Joe.

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:18.400
And we have another question
from Zoom from Juan Wang

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.150
who wants to know if
there's any data showing

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:25.093
what the lungs looked like
right before the reinfection?

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.600
- That would be a good question.

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.590
And to be honest, if I was
reviewing this piece of work

00:01:33.590 --> 00:01:35.550
as a peer reviewer,

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.750
it's probably something
I would ask the authors

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:39.950
if they had and if they could produce.

00:01:39.950 --> 00:01:42.843
They did not produce it in
this particular experiment,

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.670
so we're not sure about that.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:51.030
If I was speculating, I
would say that probably

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:54.170
the lungs had somewhat returned to normal,

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.770
inflammation like this can
take quite a while to go down.

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.010
So it wouldn't surprise me

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:00.330
if they didn't look perfectly normal,

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:02.940
but I would expect them
to suddenly have modified

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:05.910
from the extreme sort
of disease that they had

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.410
in the middle of an infection.

00:02:09.210 --> 00:02:12.970
- Great, so I can also imagine that

00:02:13.916 --> 00:02:16.080
this is a primate research project,

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:18.520
which is also a limiting factor.

00:02:18.520 --> 00:02:20.470
So we have to take that very seriously.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.900
Another question from Anissa on Zoom.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.180
How are the viral loads
on the swabs measured?

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:31.440
Also the antibodies,
how are they measured?

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:35.410
So maybe we could talk a little
bit about maybe the RT-PCR

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:38.789
and the antibody testing mechanisms.

00:02:38.789 --> 00:02:42.270
- Yeah, our colleague
Odaelys Walwyn-Pollard

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:45.230
has done a wonderful
D4P talk on antibodies

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.100
and antibody testing.

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.210
So if you're really interested in that,

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:50.670
I highly recommend you check it out,

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.070
she's a wonderful scientist.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.340
How the virus is detected is we look

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:59.030
for it's genetic material,

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:04.030
and the genetic material that
you and I have is called DNA.

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:05.980
That's the form the
genetic material takes.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:09.400
The virus produces genetic
material in a different form,

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.497
it's known as RNA.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:14.760
And what we do is we
turn that RNA into DNA,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.460
we use an enzyme, a
biological machine to do that.

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:20.670
And then we use that DNA

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:23.760
because the converted DNA
is easy for us to work with,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:26.120
and we basically look
for the presence of that.

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:28.760
We amplify it, we duplicate it,

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:31.430
and then look to see if we can detect it.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:35.280
So that's broadly speaking how we look for

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.010
the virus in those swaps.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.960
It's actually an extremely
sensitive technique.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.327
The way that these experiments are done

00:03:43.327 --> 00:03:45.250
and it's the same kind of technology

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:49.673
that underlies detecting
DNA at a crime scene.

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:52.970
We're using this amplification process

00:03:52.970 --> 00:03:57.170
to detect even really
small amounts of viral RNA

00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.960
to enable us to get quite a good measure

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.023
of how much virus that was in the lungs.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:08.930
- Great, okay, so we have
another question now from Kaitlin

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.720
who asks, if the length of
time that immunity lasts

00:04:12.715 --> 00:04:15.830
for one strain of SARS-CoV-2 is different

00:04:15.830 --> 00:04:17.203
from another strain.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:20.220
Apparently Kaitlin has
read that some strains

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:22.460
could affect people as
severely than others.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.963
What do you think about this?

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.940
- Well, so I wanna be careful
about using the word strain.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:33.330
So, scientists mean
something quite precise

00:04:33.330 --> 00:04:35.700
when they talk about different
strains of the virus.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.810
So all the different copies of the virus,

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:40.010
much like all the different
people in the world

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:42.210
are slightly different probably, right?

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:45.530
Because, there were mistakes
made when duplicating

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:47.200
when copying the virus.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:50.230
So, new strains of a virus

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:52.930
or actually what that really means is that

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.740
it has a different sort
of disease progress.

00:04:55.740 --> 00:04:57.250
It's materially different in terms of

00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:02.003
how the disease progresses,
the properties of the disease.

00:05:02.900 --> 00:05:06.320
I'm not actually that aware of there being

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:09.987
definitively different
strains of COVID-19,

00:05:09.987 --> 00:05:12.670
I'd be interested in what
you, Jeanie and Pedro

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.313
think about that.

00:05:14.313 --> 00:05:16.263
So that's the first thing that I'd say.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:21.000
- I think according to
a lot of the studies

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:24.760
there is definite a lot
of the viral researchers

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:29.570
studying this process right
now have demonstrated that

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.930
there is probably only one strain,

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:35.920
but obviously this is an RNA virus,

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.680
RNA and viruses don't have checkpoints

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:39.850
in their polymerases

00:05:39.850 --> 00:05:44.280
though the Coronavirus
family has a rudimentary

00:05:44.280 --> 00:05:47.040
proofreading capacity in their RNA base.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:51.120
So that's one of the reasons
why the Coronavirus genome

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:55.060
is remarkably large
compared to other viruses.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:58.110
But getting back to the
question of strains,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.890
so I think the consensus
among researchers is that

00:06:00.890 --> 00:06:03.730
there is a single strain
but mutations are occurring

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.330
because mutations are very natural

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:06.740
and they just occur.

00:06:06.740 --> 00:06:11.550
And so you can trace the movement

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:16.440
of the virus geographically by
assessing the tiny mutations

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:19.210
that might exist within a specific strain.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:22.080
So I don't think that
there's any data to suggest

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:23.990
that any of these mutations

00:06:23.990 --> 00:06:28.460
are leading to a different enough virus

00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:32.440
from the original virus that
started to infect humans.

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.700
And that's really relevant
because the affinity

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:36.940
for binding the ACE-2 receptor,

00:06:36.940 --> 00:06:40.300
I believe has not yet
been shown to be different

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.090
among the particles that demonstrate

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:45.590
these tiny little mutations
that might be associated

00:06:45.590 --> 00:06:48.680
with sort of the geographical locations.

00:06:48.680 --> 00:06:50.560
I hope that was clear.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.780
And I'm going to share an
article that was written

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:56.050
by a fantastic journalist, Ed Yong,

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:58.850
who describes this in more detail.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.120
I highly recommend

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:04.260
that you follow Ed Yong's
coronavirus coverage.

00:07:04.260 --> 00:07:05.260
It's really excellent.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:06.580
- I completely agree.

00:07:06.580 --> 00:07:07.430
I love Ed Yong,

00:07:07.430 --> 00:07:11.310
but just also to the aspect of immunity,

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:14.443
even if there are some mutations
found in the coronavirus,

00:07:15.416 --> 00:07:18.130
the immunity is generated
as Joe explained so well

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:21.550
on the stickiness of these
antibodies that are going to bind

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.890
the proteins on the
surface of the coronavirus.

00:07:23.890 --> 00:07:25.460
And as far as I understand,

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:28.440
none of these mutations has
caused enough difference

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:32.163
in these proteins for them to
be recognized any differently.

00:07:36.800 --> 00:07:38.650
- And I think so we do have a question

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.200
that is not related to the paper

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.890
but maybe related a little
bit to this process.

00:07:42.890 --> 00:07:44.253
And so,

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:50.640
why is it so hard to develop
a vaccine for COVID-19?

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.270
And so there's currently no
vaccine for the 2003 SARS,

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.730
and I wonder if that's
because it was contained

00:07:57.730 --> 00:08:00.470
and is now technically extinct.

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:03.630
- Yeah, I would actually
counter by saying,

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.710
I think relatively
speaking now we don't know,

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:09.420
we're still finding out
stuff about this virus,

00:08:09.420 --> 00:08:11.720
but relatively speaking,
I would actually speculate

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:15.240
that this is a virus which
it probably will be possible,

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.100
probably will be possible to
generate a vaccine against.

00:08:18.100 --> 00:08:20.440
And that's actually I'm
pretty sure that's the opinion

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:24.170
of Anthony Fauci who's the
top infectious disease expert

00:08:25.168 --> 00:08:26.590
here in the United States.

00:08:26.590 --> 00:08:30.030
There are other viruses
like HIV for example,

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.930
which scientists have been
trying for decades and decades

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:34.850
to generate viruses against,

00:08:34.850 --> 00:08:37.330
and that's actually
proved really difficult.

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.790
So I think the answer
to this question really

00:08:40.790 --> 00:08:44.003
is about people's expectations and timing.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:47.090
There is a wonderful article
in the New York Times

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.440
which lays out the
timeline for how vaccines

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:50.670
are usually developed.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:53.560
And it usually takes 20, 30, 40 years

00:08:53.560 --> 00:08:56.310
to develop a vaccine against a virus.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.440
And that's usually no small part

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:00.970
because of the different sort of testing

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:04.740
that we have to do on
the vaccine to make sure

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:05.573
that it's safe,

00:09:05.573 --> 00:09:06.780
to make sure that it works.

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:08.783
And those tests take a long time.

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.890
And the basic research to find out

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.210
what is the best version of the virus

00:09:14.210 --> 00:09:16.430
or fragment of the virus
to use in the vaccine

00:09:16.430 --> 00:09:18.020
takes a long time.

00:09:18.020 --> 00:09:20.010
So actually I think what we're gonna see

00:09:20.010 --> 00:09:22.790
probably in this case is a vaccine

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:25.010
or multiple different
vaccine candidates developed

00:09:25.010 --> 00:09:26.840
at breakneck speed,

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:29.670
relative to how long
vaccines usually take.

00:09:29.670 --> 00:09:33.240
It's just that this
virus did really appear

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.670
and catch us flat-footed.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:38.777
We hadn't done that much
research on this group of viruses

00:09:38.777 --> 00:09:40.010
and we don't have

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:44.900
the progression of those
trials already happening.

00:09:44.900 --> 00:09:46.623
We have to start from nowhere.

00:09:47.650 --> 00:09:50.670
- I also want to clarify that
my colleagues have informed me

00:09:50.670 --> 00:09:53.130
that there was indeed a
vaccine that was developed

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.533
for the original SARS in 2003,

00:09:56.570 --> 00:09:58.920
and they stopped making it

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:02.630
because the virus--

00:10:02.630 --> 00:10:03.800
- It kind of went away, yeah.

00:10:03.800 --> 00:10:04.853
- Yeah, it went away.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.793
- Okay, so next question from Danielle.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:13.820
What is it about different viruses

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:18.450
that causes variable timeframes
of memory expiration?

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:19.560
That's a really good question.

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:22.000
So for examples, the example you gave

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:25.003
of measles against
influence or common colds.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:27.920
- Yeah, this is a really good question.

00:10:27.920 --> 00:10:30.670
And I think the answer,

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.780
we know some reasons
why this might happen,

00:10:33.780 --> 00:10:36.120
but we probably don't
have the full picture yet

00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:36.953
as scientists.

00:10:36.953 --> 00:10:38.850
So if I think about some of the things

00:10:38.850 --> 00:10:40.130
that we think about

00:10:40.130 --> 00:10:42.100
and we think of as impacting how well

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.420
your immune system
works and but something,

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:47.310
usually people who have
more severe disease

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.330
and have maybe that
really debilitatingly ill

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:52.410
with the disease

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:54.640
will remember that disease
better than someone

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:56.240
who was only mildly sick.

00:10:56.240 --> 00:10:58.270
So there seems to be some correlation

00:10:58.270 --> 00:10:59.850
between how sick you get

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:02.690
and how well your immune
system is gonna remember it.

00:11:02.690 --> 00:11:05.660
Also may depend on the amount of virus

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:08.320
that your pathogen you're
exposed to at the beginning

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:11.240
and there are lots of other things

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:12.370
that probably we don't know.

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.020
Some viruses, so flu is a
really interesting virus.

00:11:15.020 --> 00:11:17.550
There's actually multiple
different types of flu

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:20.050
and multiple different strains of flu.

00:11:20.050 --> 00:11:23.440
And so we think of flu
as being a capsule term,

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:27.009
but really when you get flu the next year,

00:11:27.009 --> 00:11:28.930
it's not actually the same virus

00:11:28.930 --> 00:11:31.190
that's infecting you for a second time.

00:11:31.190 --> 00:11:34.520
It's probably a different
strain of the virus.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:37.530
So flu is very difficult to contain

00:11:37.530 --> 00:11:39.250
and remember for your immune system

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:42.470
because it's basically like
someone that keeps changing

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:43.970
their clothes, right?

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:45.830
Your immune system has
to recognize this person

00:11:45.830 --> 00:11:47.830
and it thought, "Oh, okay,
I remember this person.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.330
They were wearing red
pants and a blue shirt."

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:52.310
And then sort of comes in the next time

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:54.620
and it's wearing a yellow
shirt and green pants.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:56.850
And your system has to recognize it again.

00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:59.290
And basically has to
relearn everything again.

00:11:59.290 --> 00:12:02.743
So that's why flu particularly
is not very well remembered.

00:12:03.740 --> 00:12:05.580
- Yeah, I think that that also answers one

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:08.580
of the questions we
had from Octavia around

00:12:08.580 --> 00:12:11.663
what makes a virus more memorable.

00:12:15.481 --> 00:12:16.314
It's really tricky,

00:12:16.314 --> 00:12:18.380
and I think our colleague at Rockefeller,

00:12:18.380 --> 00:12:22.070
John Laurent Casanova,
talks about host factors.

00:12:22.070 --> 00:12:23.580
So the factors that we contain,

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:26.970
genetic differences in
our bodies that could also

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.890
dictate our immune memory

00:12:31.890 --> 00:12:33.670
or our immune responses in general.

00:12:33.670 --> 00:12:37.313
And so all of this is
really just an amazing,

00:12:39.439 --> 00:12:41.290
I think it's still kind of like a mystery

00:12:41.290 --> 00:12:42.580
for many scientists out there.

00:12:42.580 --> 00:12:44.010
It's the million dollar question.

00:12:44.010 --> 00:12:48.600
And I think the COVID-19 situation

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:50.940
that we're currently in right now

00:12:50.940 --> 00:12:54.660
is definitely driving the research

00:12:54.660 --> 00:12:57.300
on a lot of these questions in ways

00:12:57.300 --> 00:12:58.990
that we've never seen before.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:02.120
So I think a lot of this
might come out in the future.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:04.520
I mean, at least I feel positive

00:13:05.511 --> 00:13:07.790
that we are gonna make a lot
of progress in these areas

00:13:07.790 --> 00:13:10.290
given the amount of attention
it's being provided.

00:13:11.590 --> 00:13:15.780
So, we have another question from Wan

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:19.803
about the livelihood of the monkeys.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.540
Did monkeys one and two pass away

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:27.040
or were they just used as a control?

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.293
Why weren't they not reinfected?

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:34.280
- So the second part of your question

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:37.900
whether used as a control
that's really important.

00:13:37.900 --> 00:13:41.660
So scientists definitely need that.

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:42.960
So if you're gonna make a comment

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:46.650
about how the monkey
responded to reinfection,

00:13:46.650 --> 00:13:48.290
you need to be able to
compare it to a monkey

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:49.970
that was not reinfected.

00:13:49.970 --> 00:13:51.840
And the reason for that is,

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.220
let's say you reinfected
the monkey 28 days

00:13:54.220 --> 00:13:56.210
after the first infection,

00:13:56.210 --> 00:13:59.410
it's possible that there
was just a delayed response

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:01.370
which took 28 days

00:14:01.370 --> 00:14:06.060
and it's only gonna
appear after you've done

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:07.840
the second infection.

00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:11.220
And you might then wrongly
attribute that response

00:14:11.220 --> 00:14:13.050
to the re-infection when actually

00:14:13.050 --> 00:14:16.860
it was just a delayed response
from the first infection.

00:14:16.860 --> 00:14:19.913
So it's very important to
have controls and certainly,

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:24.233
that's true of the monkeys that
were already infected once.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:29.573
In answer to the overall like
livelihood at the monkeys,

00:14:30.528 --> 00:14:32.610
there were definitely
unfortunately monkeys

00:14:32.610 --> 00:14:35.363
which died in the course
of this experiment.

00:14:36.860 --> 00:14:40.260
I kind try to give an outline

00:14:40.260 --> 00:14:43.120
of the sorts of
considerations that are given

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:46.780
to an experiment like this
even being allowed to happen

00:14:46.780 --> 00:14:47.913
in the first place,

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:50.760
and there will have been a good number.

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:53.580
I can't speak to the process
at the particular institution

00:14:53.580 --> 00:14:54.930
where this paper was done.

00:14:54.930 --> 00:14:56.650
I know it's an accredited institution,

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.360
but at Rockefeller, there's a panel

00:14:59.360 --> 00:15:01.357
which consists of the generians

00:15:01.357 --> 00:15:03.950
and it has outside people
who are not affiliated

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:04.910
with the university on it.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:08.600
You have to approve every
animal experiment that's done

00:15:08.600 --> 00:15:11.300
and sign off on it and
say this is acceptable.

00:15:11.300 --> 00:15:13.917
And we use those As,
the three As to guide us

00:15:13.917 --> 00:15:15.600
in our use of animals.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:19.810
And we only choose to use
an animal like a monkey

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:24.119
when it really is necessary
for advancing the science.

00:15:24.119 --> 00:15:26.590
I don't know if Pedro or
Jeanie have anything else

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.060
to say about that?

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:30.070
- I think that's pretty much covers

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:32.430
a lot of my expertise around this.

00:15:32.430 --> 00:15:36.190
I have not ever worked
in a primate lab myself,

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.960
but I do know people
think extremely seriously.

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:43.020
These are very carefully planned

00:15:43.020 --> 00:15:46.120
and scientists go to extreme
measures to make sure

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:50.610
they're using the fewest
amount of animals possible

00:15:50.610 --> 00:15:54.540
to get the results that are rigorous

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:59.163
and applicable to whatever
disease it is that they need to,

00:16:00.640 --> 00:16:03.160
for example, the COVID-19
in this case, right?

00:16:03.160 --> 00:16:06.540
And so these are hard questions

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:08.590
and I think that I appreciate that people

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.250
are exploring this year,

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:16.090
because we are in a situation
where we can't take humans

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:19.220
and re-infect them to see what happens.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:22.110
Well, there are people who
are signing up for this

00:16:22.110 --> 00:16:23.133
kind of stuff,

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:29.400
so it's a difficult decision
that we have to make.

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:35.130
- Yeah, then you study
the disease as it goes

00:16:35.130 --> 00:16:38.050
in hospitals and with the
patients you have access to.

00:16:38.050 --> 00:16:39.700
But obviously that's not the same thing

00:16:39.700 --> 00:16:43.110
as having everything controlled
in a time controlled manner

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:44.700
in a laboratory setting,

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:46.740
which you do with monkeys,

00:16:46.740 --> 00:16:49.070
which you obviously have to reduce

00:16:49.070 --> 00:16:50.980
to the minimum possible amount.

00:16:50.980 --> 00:16:54.580
But that actually leads us very nicely

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:57.040
into our next question from Luke,

00:16:57.040 --> 00:17:01.180
who wants to know whether the
lung biopsies that you showed

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:05.270
were biopsies or were post-mortem slides,

00:17:05.270 --> 00:17:09.720
and also what your view is
on the monkey immune response

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:12.140
as compared to the human immune response

00:17:12.140 --> 00:17:15.270
to different strains of COVID-19.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:18.450
- Yeah, so that second
part is of consideration

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:23.450
that every single biologist
is worrying at night about,

00:17:23.890 --> 00:17:25.920
about whether that particular animal

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:29.500
is the same as a human, for example.

00:17:29.500 --> 00:17:30.820
But lemme address the first one first.

00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:33.490
So those were post-mortem slides done

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:36.430
after an autopsy on the monkey.

00:17:36.430 --> 00:17:37.650
But the second part

00:17:38.920 --> 00:17:41.650
with respect to how similar
is a monkey's immune system

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:42.483
to a human,

00:17:42.483 --> 00:17:45.200
I mean, the reason this
experiment was done on monkeys is

00:17:45.200 --> 00:17:48.090
because monkeys have the
most similar immune system

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:49.370
to humans.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:51.380
And so in a situation like this

00:17:51.380 --> 00:17:55.680
where we have an extremely
important time sensitive question

00:17:55.680 --> 00:17:57.940
about a pandemic that's killed hundreds

00:17:57.940 --> 00:17:59.270
of thousands of people,

00:17:59.270 --> 00:18:02.330
we want to get an answer
which is translatable

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:04.153
to humans as quickly as possible,

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:07.810
with as high degree of
certainty as possible.

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:10.470
And so that's why we would use
a monkey in this situation.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:13.420
They do have the most similar
immune system to humans

00:18:13.420 --> 00:18:17.273
as other animals that we could use.

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.220
So I would say

00:18:21.706 --> 00:18:26.240
we know that they are extremely similar,

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:29.200
there will be some modest differences.

00:18:29.200 --> 00:18:33.100
And we do our best to work those out

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:36.600
and apply what's learned
in monkeys to humans

00:18:36.600 --> 00:18:40.870
with enough modesty that
we don't think we know all

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.600
the answers and we acknowledge
that it is possible

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:45.200
but it would behave slightly
differently in a human.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:49.700
- So we have a very smart
question from Aaron,

00:18:49.700 --> 00:18:51.380
around immunological memory.

00:18:51.380 --> 00:18:54.940
So going back to that aspect,

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:59.940
and Joe, or if anybody else
on the panel of scientists

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.950
are here and maybe you would chime in

00:19:02.950 --> 00:19:06.920
if Joe, this might be outside
of your area of expertise.

00:19:06.920 --> 00:19:10.640
So in terms of the immune system's memory,

00:19:10.640 --> 00:19:15.410
is this related to how
path pathogens mutate?

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:17.950
For example, is the reason our
immune systems don't remember

00:19:17.950 --> 00:19:19.240
the common cold infections,

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.590
but do remember measles infections

00:19:21.590 --> 00:19:24.780
because the cold virus
mutates a lot more quickly,

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.580
but the measles virus does not.

00:19:27.580 --> 00:19:30.630
And how quickly is the novel
Coronavirus mutating relative

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.603
to other viruses and our immune system?

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:38.280
- Okay, so the answer
to the first part about

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:40.890
whether memory is related to mutation,

00:19:40.890 --> 00:19:43.200
it definitely is related to mutation,

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:45.700
but that's also not the full story.

00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:48.370
So for example,

00:19:48.370 --> 00:19:49.670
HIV is a virus

00:19:49.670 --> 00:19:54.670
which is extremely promiscuous
in how it replicates itself,

00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:56.630
and as a result it makes lots of mistakes

00:19:56.630 --> 00:19:57.980
and you take a huge amount

00:19:57.980 --> 00:20:00.930
and that's why it's very
difficult for your immune system

00:20:00.930 --> 00:20:01.883
to respond to it.

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:05.110
And I was just talking about flu

00:20:05.110 --> 00:20:06.570
and I use the analogy of the flu

00:20:06.570 --> 00:20:08.660
sort of changing clothes every year

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:10.430
and making it difficult
for your immune system

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:11.263
to recognize it.

00:20:11.263 --> 00:20:13.800
So that definitely has a role in shaping

00:20:13.800 --> 00:20:16.653
how well a particular
pathogen is remembered.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:21.370
But I do also want to say that

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:23.260
it doesn't explain it all.

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.590
There are definitely pathogens

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:25.940
which for whatever reason seem

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:28.770
to elicit extremely
strong immune responses

00:20:28.770 --> 00:20:31.890
in certain people and be
remembered extremely well,

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:34.660
and other pathogens which may
be a less easy to remember.

00:20:34.660 --> 00:20:36.790
And that might also be to
do with how sort of sticky

00:20:36.790 --> 00:20:38.000
the pathogens are.

00:20:38.000 --> 00:20:40.680
So if you have a pathogen
which is extremely sticky

00:20:40.680 --> 00:20:42.410
and it's very easy to make antibodies,

00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:44.680
which will stick extremely well to it,

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.190
maybe it will be remembered very well.

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:49.940
But if you have a pathogen
which is a bit slippery

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:51.780
and it's quite hard to stick to,

00:20:51.780 --> 00:20:53.730
that one might be remembered less well.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:57.160
I don't know if other people,

00:20:57.160 --> 00:20:58.620
and they're definitely
people on this panel

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:00.270
who know more about this than me.

00:21:04.380 --> 00:21:06.350
Oh, sorry, in response to the second bit,

00:21:06.350 --> 00:21:08.290
I think Jeanie already mentioned this.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:13.230
The Coronavirus actually
relative to other viruses,

00:21:13.230 --> 00:21:15.370
which are RNA viruses,

00:21:15.370 --> 00:21:16.860
it mutates quite slowly.

00:21:16.860 --> 00:21:19.210
And that's because it
has this ability to check

00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.210
whether it's doing a
good job in replicating.

00:21:22.210 --> 00:21:24.480
Check the fidelity of it's copying

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:26.450
of its genetic information.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:28.620
So when it's duplicating
its genetic information,

00:21:28.620 --> 00:21:30.030
if it makes a mistake,

00:21:30.030 --> 00:21:32.797
there is a piece of
machinery which can say,

00:21:32.797 --> 00:21:33.790
"no, you've made a mistake.

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:36.330
Chop that bit out and redo it."

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:38.410
And other RNA viruses don't have that.

00:21:38.410 --> 00:21:40.120
So they make spelling mistakes

00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:42.740
when they're replicating
the genom more often.

00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:45.923
So relatively to those viruses,
it mutates less quickly.

00:21:50.250 --> 00:21:52.040
- Well, thank you.

00:21:52.040 --> 00:21:55.130
We do have another question
from an anonymous attendee,

00:21:55.130 --> 00:21:58.120
which speaks a little bit to
what we were discussing earlier

00:21:58.120 --> 00:22:00.140
about experiments with monkey packs

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:02.990
and the differences from humans.

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:06.330
And they ask if you
know whether the monkeys

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:08.670
were all hanging out together
for this experiments?

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:11.100
Meaning, where the monkeys separated?

00:22:11.100 --> 00:22:13.910
And would this have an effect
on how sick the monkeys got

00:22:13.910 --> 00:22:15.810
or how effectively they became immune?

00:22:16.740 --> 00:22:19.020
- I mean, I think this
is a fantastic question

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.470
because one of the other
things that gets out is,

00:22:24.786 --> 00:22:26.480
one of the problems with this disease

00:22:26.480 --> 00:22:30.110
is you can't treat all
people as individuals,

00:22:30.110 --> 00:22:34.556
people associate in groups,
for example, in a family unit.

00:22:34.556 --> 00:22:37.570
So, it's quite likely that
if one of those people

00:22:37.570 --> 00:22:39.300
in those family unit gets infected,

00:22:39.300 --> 00:22:40.920
it's gonna spread around to other people.

00:22:40.920 --> 00:22:43.680
So there are definitely
dynamics that come into play

00:22:43.680 --> 00:22:48.120
when you start thinking
about how organisms

00:22:48.120 --> 00:22:50.730
or humans or monkeys grouped together.

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:52.200
I can tell you in this experiment,

00:22:52.200 --> 00:22:53.700
the monkeys would not have been allowed

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:55.150
to be in a group together

00:22:55.150 --> 00:22:57.870
because if one of the monkeys gets better,

00:22:57.870 --> 00:23:00.400
we don't want it to
confound the experiment

00:23:01.961 --> 00:23:03.640
by being reinfected by
one of the other ones

00:23:03.640 --> 00:23:05.010
which is still sick.

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:08.140
And so they will be housed separately,

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.060
and in monkey kind of housing,

00:23:10.060 --> 00:23:13.420
they usually have toys, they have mirrors,

00:23:13.420 --> 00:23:14.880
they have things like that,

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:17.550
which it's called enrichment.

00:23:17.550 --> 00:23:20.913
Basically to make the monkey
happy and give it stuff to do.

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:24.320
And so that's the sort
of conditions under which

00:23:24.320 --> 00:23:25.593
they're usually housed.

00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:31.470
But again, I don't even work
with any animals in the lab.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.490
I only work with cells in Petri dishes,

00:23:34.860 --> 00:23:38.343
I'm certainly not a primate researcher.

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:40.303
- Thanks Joe.

00:23:40.303 --> 00:23:42.480
Okay, so we're gonna
take one final question

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:45.490
because we are at one hour and 15 minutes

00:23:45.490 --> 00:23:47.880
but the conversations
have been so wonderful

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.690
that we were reluctant to
close it down after an hour,

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:53.190
and we are gonna end with
a question from Adidi.

00:23:53.190 --> 00:23:56.560
Adidi you have asked a
question at every single D4P.

00:23:56.560 --> 00:23:58.790
So thank you very much,

00:23:58.790 --> 00:23:59.780
I hope you're well.

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.160
It's so nice to see you on here.

00:24:01.160 --> 00:24:05.030
So Adidi was one of our SSRP students,

00:24:05.030 --> 00:24:08.200
so that's exciting that
you're joining us here.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.450
Okay, this is a fantastic question

00:24:10.450 --> 00:24:13.740
around male, female differences

00:24:13.740 --> 00:24:15.890
and potential role of hormones.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:18.440
And so as the research indicated
the female immune system

00:24:18.440 --> 00:24:21.140
is better than fighting the
outbreak due to differences

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:21.973
in hormone levels.

00:24:21.973 --> 00:24:25.380
I'm not sure if the
research has indicated,

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:30.380
I know that females tend to get COVID-19

00:24:30.670 --> 00:24:33.770
at a lower frequency than males,

00:24:33.770 --> 00:24:35.810
but it's not clear that's due to hormones.

00:24:35.810 --> 00:24:37.340
So I just wanna throw that out there,

00:24:37.340 --> 00:24:39.590
but I love the way that
you're thinking here.

00:24:40.910 --> 00:24:44.003
Do you know if hormones play
any role in immune memory?

00:24:45.570 --> 00:24:48.090
- So this is totally
over my head, I do not,

00:24:48.090 --> 00:24:50.740
but let me make one comment.

00:24:50.740 --> 00:24:54.243
Which is that, in relation
to this hormone question,

00:24:54.243 --> 00:24:58.780
it's very difficult to tease
out the different parts

00:24:58.780 --> 00:25:01.250
of what's having an impact in science,

00:25:01.250 --> 00:25:02.900
particularly in human experiments.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:04.660
Because we know for example,

00:25:04.660 --> 00:25:07.250
that men smoke more often than women.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:10.980
And smoking is obviously gonna
be pretty bad for your lungs.

00:25:10.980 --> 00:25:14.560
And it's a risk factor for
having a worst prognosis

00:25:14.560 --> 00:25:16.910
if you get a respiratory disease.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:21.910
So seeing that man are
getting a particular disease,

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:26.330
worse and have worse
outcomes as a result of it,

00:25:26.330 --> 00:25:29.920
might be to do with biological variation

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:31.990
caused by hormones

00:25:31.990 --> 00:25:35.350
or it could be to do with other things

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:39.930
which also are to do with being male.

00:25:39.930 --> 00:25:42.420
So if men smoke at a high rate,

00:25:42.420 --> 00:25:44.260
then really what's happening is it's not

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:49.080
that being male is causing
you to develop COVID worse,

00:25:49.080 --> 00:25:52.020
it's that smoking is causing
you to develop COVID worse

00:25:52.020 --> 00:25:56.140
and smoking as a factor correlates

00:25:57.170 --> 00:25:59.170
with sex somehow.

00:25:59.170 --> 00:26:01.070
So we have to be very careful

00:26:01.070 --> 00:26:02.660
when thinking about those things.

00:26:02.660 --> 00:26:06.270
I personally do not know
anything about hormones

00:26:06.270 --> 00:26:07.860
and their impacts on the immune system,

00:26:07.860 --> 00:26:09.330
but I don't know if one
of the other panelists

00:26:09.330 --> 00:26:11.660
can shed some light on that.

00:26:11.660 --> 00:26:14.410
- Immunology is definitely over my head.

00:26:14.410 --> 00:26:16.277
I'm just a plain old geneticist so

00:26:16.277 --> 00:26:18.527
(laughing)

00:26:19.983 --> 00:26:22.820
I think Joe what you've
raised here is sort of like

00:26:25.444 --> 00:26:28.830
the problems that we face in the lab

00:26:28.830 --> 00:26:31.220
or in clinical studies, in the field

00:26:32.140 --> 00:26:37.140
it's really hard to boil
something down to a single reason.

00:26:37.489 --> 00:26:38.990
- Exactly.

00:26:38.990 --> 00:26:42.130
- [Jeanie] Yeah, and
so it's multifactorial

00:26:42.130 --> 00:26:43.770
and I think there's gonna many years

00:26:43.770 --> 00:26:47.180
of research being done
to really fully give us

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:49.603
any kind of a picture moving forward.

00:26:50.679 --> 00:26:55.010
And it's Dr. Pollard who is
on here is just texting us

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:57.440
to tell us it's really
hard to tease out hormones

00:26:57.440 --> 00:26:59.590
and their impact on the immune system.

00:26:59.590 --> 00:27:00.993
And yeah,

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:05.960
it's a hard question

00:27:05.960 --> 00:27:08.900
but I appreciate that people
are thinking in that direction

